CLUE Newsletter, October 1996
===============================

This Month's Topic: 

	Introduction to CDE - The Common Desktop Environment
	by Jeremy Chatfield, X Inside Inc.

   Based on X11R5 and Motif 1.2, CDE is an attempt by a number of 
	companies to provide a common graphical interface for the Unix 
	desktop, including system administration and printing.

	Jeremy will speak on the following issues:
	- Why was CDE developed?
	- What is in CDE? - a demonstration will be given
	- What is the likely future of CDE?

   Jeremy Chatfield has a long history with Unix systems, 
   including managing the Dell Unix group back in the early 90's.

Next Month's Topic:
	Next months topic has not been determined at the time of this
	writing.  When information on the topic becomes available a new
	newsletter will be sent out.

Future Events:
   Keep an eye out for our Linux Installation Workshops, to be
   announced soon!

===========================

Notes from September meeting:
	Randy Bentson, author of "Inside Linux" (published by SSC, publishers
	of Linux Journal and its Web-based counterpart, the Linux Gazette)
	spoke on how Internet-based cooperative development built something
	better than what IBM and Microsoft have been able to do, namely
	Linux.

	Randy's talk was lively and informative and was accompanied by a 
	question and answer period with very good audience participation.
	CLUE looks forward to hosting other authors and well known developers
	from the Linux community in the future.


	PCTV "USER GROUP" covers Unix and Linux!
	----------------------------------------
	The PCTV production company (http://www.pctv.com) which produces Computer
	Chronicles, USER GROUP and @HOME is producing four half-hour shows on UNIX
	and Linux.  These shows are:
	
	  o History of UNIX
	  o Contemporary UNIX
	  o Linux
	  o UNIX Futures
	
	These shows will be aired as part of the USER GROUP show, which is carried
	on ME/U (15 million households in the U.S., Canada, Mexico and England),
	Jones Computer Network (a 24-hour cable network dedicated entirely to the
	subject of computers) and the NBC Super Channel (69 million homes in 44
	countries) and CNBC Europe (7.2 million homes), as well as by Satellite.
	USIA WorldNet will begin carrying the Users Group show.  It is going to be
	broadcasted to 280 countries and translated into 17 languages.
	
	Please check your local cable or satellite company for viewing times of
	USER GROUP, but the first show is airing under this schedule:
	
	The first show has already aired but will be repeated
	on JCN and MEU the week of October 21st, 1996 at:
	
	Mon.  9:30 PM - 10:00 PM
	Tue. 11:30 PM - 12:00 AM
	Wed. 11:30 PM - 12:00 AM
	Thr.  9:30 PM - 10:00 PM
	Fri. 11:30 PM - 12:00 AM
	Sat.  9:30 PM - 10:00 PM
	
	Other shows will follow later in the season.  Please check the PCTV Home
	Page for dates/times and to let them know you appreciate these shows.
	
	The original posting from comp.os.linux.announce will be posted to the CLUE
	Newsletter mailing list in a seperate posting.


   X Inside is hiring!
   -------------------

   The guys from X Inside stopped by in August to mention that they're
   hiring!  There are a number of positions open, some entry level,
   some with more experience required.  X Inside develops and sells
   various components for X Windows environments on multiple Unix
   platforms.  You can contact them at http://www.xinside.com or
   try sales@xinside.com.  X Inside is located in Denver, Colorado.


   Linux Gazette is looking for writers!
   -------------------------------------

   Marjorie Richardson at SSC is now the managing editor for the
   Linux Gazette.  Majorie has been working with Michael Hammel
   (Clue Newsletter writer) on his article for Linux Journal (see
   the Novemember Issue of Linux Journal) and asked that he spread
   the word that she's looking for articles for the Linux Gazette.
   This is a free, online magazine.  If you've got an idea for an
   article, a neat trick or tip for Linux, or just some useful
   configuration of a popular tool, send email to Marjorie!  You can
   check out the Linux Gazette at http://www.ssc.com/lg/index.html
   or send email to gazette@ssc.com.
   
===========================

Administrivia:

CLUE is interested in organizing a booth at local trade shows.  Would
   you be interested in this?  Would you participate?  Come to the October
   meeting and let us know your thoughts on such local Linux exposure.

CLUE welcomes members of the Linux User Community to speak at the monthly
   meetings.  Please contact President@CLUE.Denver.CO.US to arrange
   your talk.  All speakers are expected to offer a written summary of
   the talk so that we may place it in the newsletter and on our web site.

CLUE has a new home page address!  
   Find us at http://www.clue.denver.co.us/~clue/
   The old address, http://www.spot.elfwerks.com/~clue, will 
   still work as well.
 
CLUE is for anyone with an interest in Linux, whether you are an
   expert or just interested and want to know more about the
   Internet and the Operating System phenomena that Linux
   represents. All interested parties are invited to attend.
   Let's learn from each other!

The Colorado Linux Users & Enthusiasts (CLUE), normally meets the
   third Thursday of every month. The next meeting will be at
   7:00pm on November 21st. The location will be Smoky Hill High
   School, located at 16100 E. Smoky Hill Rd. in Southeast
   Aurora.  CLUE extends its many thanks to Smokey Hill High
   school for the use of their facilities.

Directions to Smoky Hill High School:  Take Parker south from
   I-225, to Quincy.  There will be a shopping center on the
   left, and a Stoplight. Take a left on Quincy. Then the first
   stoplight with a right turn will be Smoky Hill Road.  Turn
   right onto Smoky Hill, cross Chambers (a light), and then
   turn right at Laredo (a stop light), then left into the
   School parking lot. Go past the main entrance, and to the
   activities entrance.  There will be signs posted in the
   building to direct you to the room where we meet.

This newsletter is compiled by Michael J. Hammel <mjhammel@csn.net>,
   who welcomes comments on its form and content.

