I would like to introduce the Laboratory for Micro Enterprise and announce
our flagship project, Computer Craftsmanship. This project aims to realise
affordable computer aided design and manufacture for craft enterprise, and
ultimately for all. 

The LME is a virtual organisation focused on the needs and difficulties of
very small enterprises. Its core purpose is to carry out generic research and
development, in technical and social areas, aimed at increasing the
competitiveness and standing of this important economic group. The LME is part
of the growing, Internet-based free software movement. I invite interested
organisations and individuals around the world to participate in our projects.
I welcome proposals for collaboration from other organisations which share our
aims. Further details on the web site below.

The Computer Craftsmanship project is based on development work in my craft
workshop over the past 15 years. That involved the design and prototype
construction of two cnc machine tools, and all machine control and part
definition software. These systems are in daily production use and produce
hundreds of different parts. The project aims to consolidate that work, and
hopes to realise for 3d artifacts what desk top publishing has for 2d. A key
requirement is an affordable cnc machine tool, and this is the focus of the
present phase of the project and the subject of our first software release. 
We have picked our winner, the LME Hexapod Machine, and produced a detailed
software model which is now freely available under the Gnu GPL from the web
site.  It is in C++ under Linux, and generates solid model output for Povray
and a wealth of geometric and force/displacement data. The software includes
artifact and primitive classes which will be the foundation of the Computer
Craftsmanship artifact modelling system. Their first test is in the machine
model. Detailed machine design drawings will be available soon, under a
GPL-like license. The project has been enabled by support from the European
Commission.

I would appreciate feedback and suggestions for collaboration. I believe that
the Computer Craftsmanship project is the first major free software effort in
its area, and that I will be breaking new ground by making mechanical designs
freely available on the net. Information about other such projects would be
welcome. I'd also like pointers to other web sites dealing with cad/cam/cim.

