-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Ever wished to

 o fax directly from the XEmacs LaTeX mode?
 o fax directly from Netscape or another MIME-capable mailer?

Here you go. tsfax which has been around for more than a year now has
been extended to work as a generic MIME-to-fax gateway. It works
smoothly with Gert Doering's mgetty+sendfax package, i.e. with the
"sendfax" part.

tsfax consists of three parts: 

 o tex-send-fax -- a XEmacs latex-mode extension (described in iX
   Multiuser Multitasking Magazine in the June 1995 issue,
   pp. 190). tex-send-fax can handle an arbitrary number of appendices in
   the formats that faxspool knows of (ASCII, PS, G3, GIF, PBM, and
   others). It allows you to set up your own header page decoupled
   from TeX - any text editor capable of exporting Postscript will
   do. You can preview and locally print this headerpage from the
   latex-mode. LaTeX knowledge is of advantage to use the bells and
   whistles LaTeX gives you.
 o faxmail -- a gateway which either processes tex-send-fax output (and
   can be called directly from tex-send-fax) or can be called from the
   command line. faxmail resembles faxspool from mgetty+sendfax a lot
   but it produces a MIME compliant file and sends it to a remote fax
   server via email. Each fax appendix is converted into a MIME
   attachment.  
 o mail2fax -- a MIME-to-fax converter which is installed as a mail
   filter alias in /etc/aliases. mail2fax re-creates the original fax
   appendices from the MIME attachments and feeds them into the local
   faxspool command. faxspool in turn sends you an email message after
   completion of its work. 

Prerequisites: 

 o mail2fax is a Perl script so you want Perl to be installed on the
   fax server (not on the client). 
 o the package uses mgetty+sendfax so you need this too on the machine
   with the modem attached. 

tsfax-0.90 is available on the "home" site of mgetty+sendfax,
ftp://ftp.leo.org/pub/comp/networking/communication/modem/mgetty
   
Have fun!

