-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

 To people interested in developing distributed systems,

 I have been developing a software for distributed programming under
Linux, called DIPC. It enables the use of UNIX system V IPC mechanisms, 
i.e. shared memories, messages, and semaphores, in a cluster of Linux 
computers made using off-the-shelf hardware. This is easy to build and very 
familiar to programmers, allowing them to use both messages and shared 
memories for their data transfers.

 DIPC is one of the few Distributed Shared Memory (DSM) systems available 
for Linux. The main parts of it run in user level, resulting in minimal 
changes being made to the kernel itself.

 Due to several problems (lack of equipment being one of them), I can not 
work on it as well as I would like to. So I am inviting interested people
to participate in the development of DIPC.

 You could consider working in any of the areas written below:
 * Writing programs and testing DIPC. 
 * Porting DIPC to Linux kernel 2.x.x.
 * Trying to use DIPC in IPX/SPX networks.
 * studying the use of DIPC in a WAN, and making any needed changes to make
   it work there.
 * Porting DIPC to non-intel architectures that are supported by Linux,
   such as M68K, PowerPC and so on.
 * Changing the DIPC's shared memory from segment-based to paged-segmented.
 * Porting DIPC to other UNIX operating systems that support System V IPC.
 * Making DIPC fault tolerant.     
 * Any other interesting ideas you may have.

 As you see, some of the above are major undertakings, and may require 
considerable effort.

 Contact me if you are interested. Unfortunately, the email system is not 
very reliable, but you can always reach me by ordinary mail. DIPC sources
can be found at sunsite.unc.edu, in /pub/Linux/ALPHA directory. A patch file
(dipc-0.15pl1) can be found at the same site in /pub/Linux/Incoming.


