        Yggdrasil has shipped the 8 CD Winter 1997 edition of Linux
Internet Archives.  The price is still $22.95 (under $3 per CD now
that the archives have grown to eight discs).  It is the first Linux
product to include Linux 2.1 sources (2.0 is included too, of course).
The Winter 1997 edition also includes nine non-Yggdrasil Linux
distributions, including the free portions of all three architectures
of the Red Hat 4.0 Linux distribution (Intel, Sparc and Alpha) for
less than the cost of buying the official version of any one of
these--a great bargain for users who don't need technical support or
don't mind paying for support separately.

	To make things easy to find, the CD's are labeled with their
contents and each CD has files listing the complete contents of all of
CD's in the set.  Here is a are the contents of the Winter 1997 Linux
Internet Archives:

        Discs 1-2: The free software on the Sunsite Linux archive,
                   including Linux ***2.1*** sources.

        Disc 3:   The free software on the tsx-11 Linux archive,
                  dilinux Linux distribution,
                  JE Japanese Extensions for Linux

        Disc 4:   Sparc, gnu, X11R6.1 distribution tar files,
                  X11R6.1 contributed software

        Disc 5:   Debian 1.1 and 1.1.1 i386 Linux distributions

        Disc 6:   The free portions of slackware 3.1 i386
                  The free portions of Red Hat 4.0 sparc (bootable CD!)
                  MIPS, PowerPC and Mach i386 Linux archive snapshots

        Disc 7:   The free portions of Red Hat 4.0 i386
                  Red Hat 4.0 source code
                  Jurix, MCC 1.0+, Internet RFC standards

        Disc 8:   Linux-Alpha archives from Digital, azstarnet and grocne,
                  Red Hat 4.0 for Digital Alpha (bootable CD!)


	In total, the Winter 1997 edition of Linux Internet Archives
contains nine non-Yggdrasil Linux distributions:

		1. BLADE 0.3 for DEC Alpha
		2. Debian 1.1 for i386
		3. Jurix
		4. MCC 1.0+
		5. mini-linux
		6. the free portions of Red Hat 4.0 for i386, sparc and alpha
		7. the free portions of Slackware 3.1 for i386
		8. Dilinux
		9. Xdenu

        Although somone once produced a 10 CD set related to Linux by
including a paper texture CD and MPEG collection CD and not removing
files duplicated between the sunsite and tsx-11 Linux archives, the 8
CD Winter 1997 edition of Linux Internet Archives is, by far, the most
comprehensive collection of Linux FTP sites ever published.

	Linux Internet Archives is intended for people who are already
familiar with Linux, and who who want to have the most up-to-date
accurate images of the Linux FTP sites at their fingertips.  Although
it is possible to install from the linux distributions included on
Linux Internet Archives and to do so substantially faster than
downloading from the internet, Linux Internet Archives is not designed
as a "Plug and Play" system.  Linux Internet Archives does not include
a boot floppy, a live filesystem, or a manual.  Technical support is
