I have uploaded version 3.0.15 of Remind to sunsite.unc.edu.  It should
appear in /pub/Linux/apps shortly; you can also get it from:

	ftp://ftp.doe.carleton.ca/pub/remind-3.0/

Remind is a sophisticated calendar/alarm package which runs under most
UN*X like OSes.  The development platform is Linux.  Some features of Remind:

- - Intelligent handling of holidays
- - Sophisticated date calculations
- - Timed pop-up reminders
- - Very nice-looking PostScript output
- - Compile-time selection of English, German, Dutch, Finnish, French,
  Norwegian, Danish, Polish, Brazilian Portuguese or Italian output.
- - Handles the Hebrew calendar
- - Calculates moon phases and sunrise/sunset times.
- - Very flexible and programmable, yet also...
- - Easy to use, especially with the new graphical front-end (requires
  X Windows and TCL/Tk.)

Remind can be distributed under a modified version of the GPL; see
the file COPYRIGHT for details.  Chances are very good that the
modifications don't affect you in the slightest.

Here is an excerpt from the WHATSNEW file, followed by the LSM
(Linux Software Map) entry.

Regards,

David F. Skoll

- ----------------------------------------------------------------------
CHANGES TO REMIND

* Version 3.0 Patch 15

+ IMPORTANT NOTES

- - The tar file now unpacks into a Remind subdirectory rather than into
  the current working directory.

- - I no longer support Remind under DOS.  I don't think I've done anything
  to stop it from working under DOS, but will no longer compile and test
  it under DOS, and can't help you if you get stuck.  Sorry -- I no longer
  have a DOS machine.

+ MINOR ENHANCEMENTS

- - Changed psshade() to accept 1 or 3 arguments for colored shading in
  PostScript calendar mode.

- - Added a Print dialog to tkremind.

- - Added support for Brazilian Portuguese courtesy of Marco Paganini

- - Added support for Italian courtesy of Valerio Aimale

+ BUG FIXES

- - Fixed confusing error in rem2ps help messages.

- - Fixed bug in TkRemind which caused a crash if the "-m" option was used
  for a month beginning on Sunday.  Doh!!!

