-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

*****************************************************************
*** F I N A L L Y   T H E   T I M E   H A S   C O M E   . . . ***
*****************************************************************
*** W e   h a v e   d a r e d   p u s h i n g   o u t   t h e ***
*****************************************************************

*****************************************************************
*** P U B L I C   P R E V I E W   V E R S I O N   O F   G G I ***
*****************************************************************

GGI is intended to be the Linux General Graphics Interface.

A few words from the README :
=============================

This is a "public preview release". It uses a much smaller kernel patch
than the final GGI will require for its advanced features.

It is intended to "give you the feel" of what GGI can do, while not
requiring you to plug a big fat patch into your kernel that will even
break some software.

GGI 0.0.8 is a mixture of the new drivers that were created by Steffen
Seeger and several others and the library, demos and kernel-interface
from the older scrdrv package by Andreas Beck.

Though our destination is to move to a completely new console scheme
to allow several advanced features like multi-console support plus
multi-io-devices support (a major step ahead for integrating devices
like braille readers and other special IO systems for handicapped
people or special purposes), we have chosen to make a special
public-preview-release that can do with only a slight kernel patch
to allow for more people to test it.

So why should I use GGI ?
=========================

Software-Authors:
- -----------------

- - You can easily port DOS applications (we tried it for Borland-BGI, DJGPP
  and several other DOS-graphics-interfaces, it was a matter of a few
  minutes in most cases).
- - Your binaries need not be suid-root (which is why I would not run an
  "untrusted" graphics binary like a game).
- - Your programs need to know NOTHING about the graphics card they run on,
  while still using it at its best. Hardware acceleration is completely
  hidden by the library.
- - Your programs benefit from new(er) drivers "on-the-fly".

Hardware-Vendors:
- -----------------

- - You could deliver the necessary drivers with your hardware just like
  with DOS and Win for the convenience of your customers.
- - Chip-Designers could make up "stock-linux-drivers".
- - Still under discussion : We _MIGHT_ allow you to build binary-only drivers
  for the protection of your design secrets.
  Please note, that this is disliked very much in the linux community.
  We will need the agreement of all source authors to place a suitable
  license on the package sources (you will definitely need them ...) .
  Public discussion of this issue is still in progress.

Users:
- ------

- - GGI will make your graphics subsystem much more stable. 
  No malfunctioning graphics application can screw up your system
  any more. 

  X hangs ? Can't even switch consoles ? Just press SAK and X will 
  be killed off CLEANLY (provided you loaded the correct driver 
  for your card, not only VGA generic) and textmode will be restored.

  DOOM crashed in the init-phase and left the keyboard in RAW-mode ?
  GGI will help you out.

- - GGI will enhance security. There are professional games binaries 
  out there (DOOM etc.) ... Though they can probably be trusted,
  wouldn't you like them better, if they wouldn't need being suid
  root ?

- - GGI will speed up most applications, as GGI transparently uses
  acceleration where available.

- - More applications will be ported, as they will have to care less
  about the graphics hardware below.


Where can I look at PP-GGI ?
============================

You can find it on my homepage :

http://sunserver1.rz.uni-duesseldorf.de/~becka

or soon on our GGI homepage:

http://synergy.caltech.edu/~ggi/

The very lastest version can be downloaded via CVS from

:pserver:guest@miango.com:/home/todd/src/ggi

Password for this account is guest. Please note that this is
"developers-only" stuff. If you do not know what CVS is, do not
try it.

Where can I get more info on GGI ?
==================================

The information on my homepage is quite out of date, though the
general concepts are still valid.

More up to date info can be found at the ggi-homepage :
http://synergy.caltech.edu/~ggi/

Why do we release this public-preview ?
=======================================

The final version will need a BIG FAT kernel patch which is not yet
accepted by the kernel folks, as we have not yet really shown what it 
will be good for.

This is a preview with only a small patch that seems to break nothing
(I haven't noticed any difficulties), so everyone can try it out.

We want to show what GGI can do, though the really "cool" effects 
like generic input-device support, multi-display support,... can
only be seen in final GGI.

The current version is limited to generic VGA and several S3 drivers
right now. Generic VGA should suffice for a test-drive (be careful
not to judge GGI by the speed of the ModeX modes, they are slow, we
know that) , though only the lucky ones with a supported S3 board 
will see how acceleration does things you wouldn't have imagined. 
(Like the S3-DOS "this is not a palette change demo ... this 
 comes from filling the screen ..." .)

What will I get when I download this 400K of source ?
=====================================================

You will get KGI-drivers (kernel loadable modules) for generic VGA plus
several S3-based cards. Trident, ATI MACH, ELSA S3-based and friends 
are under way.

You will get a small kernel patch (for the 2.0.X series) needed by the
kernel modules.

You will get a GGI-specific demo-library showing how GGI handles transparent
acceleration.

You will get a preliminary SVGAlib Emulation. Do not expect too much from
it now. This is really new.

You will get a bunch of demos showing what GGI can do and how easy it is to
program them.

The demos include : several test suites for 4,8,16,32 bpp modes 
                    (16/32 only supported on S3)
                    a mandelbrot demo with interactive capabilities
                    GGI's idea of a screensaver (works with X as well
                    as with consoles)
                    a fasttext demo showing direct screen access under
                    Linux
                    a small video titling system (fully functional only
                    with S3, can load GIFs in any case)
                    a voxel-demo : fly over a small landscape or walk
                    through some strange rooms

So what am I waiting for ?
==========================

Sorry, I dunno ... ;-)

CU,ANdy

