-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Hello,

I just uploaded my new version 0.3 of netboot to sunsite.unc.edu. It should
soon appear as /pub/Linux/system/Linux-boot/netboot-0.3.tar.gz. The new
features include:

	- it's now possible to also boot MS-DOS on the diskless client
	  (that's why this posting has been sent not only to cola, but
	  also to comp.archives.msdos.announce).
	- mknbi-linux is now able to correctly use ramdisks (thanks to
	  Markus Gutschke for his support), and has been enhanced with
	  some new features (selecting the boot device, for example).
	- easier configuration than with previous version. The whole
	  package now uses GNU's autoconf, and only uses one master
	  Makefile for all compilation tasks.
	- should now compile on any Unix-type system (not just Intel based)
	- the bootrom is now able to use more than just one DOS binary but
	  as many as will fit into 64kB besides the packet driver.
	- added a network debugging module (as a sample application for
	  the multiple-DOS-binary-feature mentioned above). You can now
	  see every single packet going through the bootrom, if you like.
	- fixed a whole bunch of bugs (and probably introduced some new
	  ones, sigh ;-))

And for all of you who don't know what netboot is all about: this package
contains most of the sources and binaries which are necessary to boot
a diskless system over a TCP/IP network. You can use it for example to
revive an old system and convert it into an X terminal, a network router,
printer spooler etc... Here are the main features of this package:

	- Booting a diskless client either from an EPROM or from a floppy
	  without additional utility tools
	- The Bootrom code can use many standard DOS packet drivers and
	  therefore supports almost any PC network card on the market.
	- The bootrom can load a BOOTP vendor extension file in order to
	  support more space for tags according to RFC 1533.
	- Cross-gateway booting
	- Bootrom runs on any 80x86 processor but is optimized for the 386+.
	- Able to boot different operating system. Currently supported are
	  Linux and MS-DOS.

gero.

