With more than a little chagrin at the stupendously long delay in doing
so, I'd like to announce that the (original?) Linux logos/images web
page has not only been updated for the first time in about six months,
but also expanded to three pages.  The original is still where it's 
always been:

    http://quest.jpl.nasa.gov/Info-ZIP/people/greg/greg_lnxpics.html

but there are now links from it to the two new pages:

    http://quest.jpl.nasa.gov/Info-ZIP/people/greg/greg_lnxpic2.html
    http://quest.jpl.nasa.gov/Info-ZIP/people/greg/greg_lnxpeng.html

The last one is where most of the new and highly spiffy contest logos
reside (and not just *&!@%$! penguins, either).  It's also the one where
I'm missing several attributions, since that information in many cases
wasn't available at the Linux logo competition site.  So if you submitted
one or know somebody who did, please check the web page and help me fill
in the anonymous entries!  And many, many apologies to those who sent me
pointers to images as far back as last April.  Oops.

Btw, please note that the web page is and always has been a reflection
of my personal taste; it is *not* intended to be an exhaustive database
of all Linux logos or Linux-related images, and if I don't include yours,
don't be offended:  it just means I have bad taste. :-)

And one final comment:  these pages have been the origin of several
popular logos that have subsequently found their way onto other web
pages, in many cases with neither attribution nor a link back to my
page where there *are* attributions.  I hate to lecture, but this is 
MOST uncool.  In almost all cases the copying is no problem--that's 
why the images were created, eh?--but leaving off the attributions 
(or a pointer to them) denies the author recognition and may even 
prevent a well-meaning company from tracking down the owner and get-
ting permission to use the logo in an ad or something.  (I'm not
particularly concerned about my own work--I've only created two logos
single-handedly.  But Pacific HiTech's beautiful, swirly Linux-ball 
logo, created by Richard Hammond, is an example of a commercial logo
that has ``gotten loose.'')

OK, I'll get off the soapbox now.  Enjoy...

- --
