I'm looking for a volunteer to take over the development of the
free X11 compressor dxpc (ftp://ftp.x.org/contrib/utilities/dxpc-3.3.0.tar.gz)

dxpc-3.3.0 is the final version of dxpc that I'm planning to
implement.  While there are a lot of improvements that could
be added to dxpc, I don't much time to devote to dxpc development
lately, and I want to move on to other projects.  Thus, I'm looking
for someone else to take over the maintenance and future development
of dxpc.

Maintaining dxpc involves:
   * Handling a small volume of mail (normally a few messages
     per week) from users who encounter problems building or
     running dxpc.

   * Porting dxpc to various systems (normally just a matter of
     incorporating patches that people send in)

   * Adding new features--there's lots of interesting stuff
     that could be implemented.  Possible performance improvements
     include:
       - Better text compression
       - Better compression of X_Poly* requests by enhancing
         dxpc to try multiple predictive models for coordinates
         and use the one that yields the shortest encoding
       - Better color image compression (the current implementation
         is really primitive, though fairly effective for something
         so simple; a better algorithm and support for image depths
         other than 8 would make dxpc useful for a wider range of
         X applications)
       - More compact encodings for the compressed data stream

      Possible usability improvements include:
       - Have the Client Proxy and Server proxy exchange
         version numbers at startup and exit if they're not
         compatible versions
       - Better integration with SSH (I suspect that modifying
         dxpc to allow the use of a UNIX domain pipe between
         the Client Proxy and Server Proxy would be a good idea...)
       - A dxpc web page and/or mailing list would be nice, as
         would precompiled dxpc distributions for common OS'es.


So, if you're interested in taking over the development of dxpc,
and you have a good working knowledge of UNIX, C++, the X protocol,
and compression (or you at least have half a clue and a desire to
learn), and you're willing and able to make your work freely
distributable for both noncommercial and commercial use, send
me some email.

Brian Pane
brianp@cnet.com

