[ Moderator's note: This has been lying in my mailbox since 21 September.
  I should have posted it sooner, but was buried in other things to do.
  My apologies to WGS. --liw ]

>>                            ...the need for SPEED?

It looks like I am going to be buried in apologies and deletions
for a while.  I did not intend spam nor general broadcast, and in 
fact we did not do so.  A few (probably less than 300) email messages
went out to people who had asked previously to be left out of our
broadcast E-Mails.  Not having the intention to spam is doubly true
because this message involved one of my suppliers, not just ourselves,
Spamming could jeapordize our relationship as X Inside does not condone 
spamming either.

This emailing *WAS* restricted to people who have communicated with 
our company at one time or another... it is not a general "collect 
the addresses and spam".  Unfortunately this included a few
mailing lists that I subscribe to which have now been added
to the suppress database.  This is one thing that might in my 
perception make us look like a spammer... but it was restricted
to only a very few lists. The other is that some people that were
supposed to be marked no-broadcast slipped through.

If someone wants proof that this is the case, I can dig out the 
communication that resulted in their email address being in our 
database.

An over enthusiastic programmer (severely chastised) took out one of 
the final check routines that suppresses addresses who have indicated 
they did not want broadcast email from us no way, nohow... so we are 
in fact sinners....  Our policy is only to send to those that want it.  

We are doing everything we can to correct this so it never occurs 
again.  Please by all means if something else ever manages to slip 
through in the future, feel justified in whatever you feel you need 
to do.  Please also be aware that if you then email us again, you 
will be added again, unless your address is also in our suppression 
of email database (which is being frantically expanded by two of my
people as I write).

Please accept my humble apology in this matter.  We *do* have tens 
of thousands of people who *HAVE* requested this sort of E-Mail 
notification.  It is difficult being perfect when one has over a
hundred thousand email addresses to maintain as to send/no send,
but at least we make the effort to do it right.

Our policy of putting people on the list if there is communication, 
between themselves and our company and then taking them off again if 
they ask it is a fair one.  Any "spam" generated was entirely 
inadvertant *NOT* intentional.

Most of our customers and associates appreciate being kept informed.

Nevertheless, I appreciate hearing from you, and your name has been 
marked "no broadcast" in the general database, and also added to the 
suppress file for "double sureity".  If we delete it outright, and
completely, then you email us again... the supression goes away.

I apologize for any inconvenience this broadcast may have caused you, 
and the many others who objected.

Mark (with red face)

PS, I helped write the original protocols that were used in the founding
of the Internet, and have been involved ever since (over 2 decades).  
I feel that my opinion of what is fair and not fair on the internet is
at least as valid as the next persons.  We did not intend to spam, but 
it looks like a programmer's mistake did in fact turn us marginally 
into a spammer, darn it. 

I do feel that it is entirely fair for us to do broadcast email to 
our customers and associates, and at the same time I hate spam as
much as anyone else.  

