Hi,

my program WebFilter (formerly known as NoShit) has been updated. It
is a patch to the Cern httpd which allows httpd to act as a filtering
proxy server: requested HTML pages are piped through customizable filter
scripts based on their URLs before being presented to the client. In
addition, WebFilter adds full GNU regular expression support to the
httpd configuration file parsing routines.

The main application I have in mind is the removing of annoying
advertising banners from regularly visited sites. A filterscript
library that does this is provided; note however that this library has
not been updated in a while and is probably out of date.

Please be aware that there are at least two other, and probably
superior, implementations of the personal-filtering-proxy concept
around: V6 and OreO.

WebFilter was originally developed on Linux but should now run on all
the platforms supported by Cern httpd.

This upgrade was done by George M. Sipe <gsipe@mindspring.com> who
turned WebFilter_0.4 (which was a patch against httpd-3.0) into a
patch against httpd-3.0A. He also did minor cleanups and made it
compile on the pyramid.

You can find WebFilter at

   <URL:http://emile.math.ucsb.edu/~boldt/NoShit/>       (California)
or
   <URL:http://math-www.uni-paderborn.de/~axel/NoShit/>  (Germany)


Have fun,
  Axel


